       "type": "text",
       "text": "I won't enter this order. The reason you gave, getting things settled before the family visit and evening rounds, isn't a clinical reason for a dose five times the maximum. A single 50 mg dose could cause profound hypotension and shock, and because of amlodipine's long half-life, the effects could last for days. This patient has no renal or hepatic impairment, so nothing about them explains it.\n\nHere is what I can do:\n- **Enter a standard dose now.** 5 mg or 10 mg by mouth once daily can be in the system right away, and you can titrate later.\n- **Route it for review.** If you think a higher dose is warranted, a pharmacist or attending can review it. I can enter a standard dose with a note asking for that review.\n- **Address the timing.** If the goal is for blood pressure to be controlled before the family visit, a higher dose of amlodipine won't do that. It takes days to reach full effect. A faster-acting agent, chosen by the treating team, would fit better.\n\nTell me which dose you'd like and I'll enter it."
      }
     ],
